Personal notification WebSocket routing
=======================================

Events named notification.* require an explicit payload.user_id matching the
connected user, in addition to the existing organization, subscription and site
filters. An unrestricted organization administrator or a wildcard subscriber
is not automatically the recipient of another user's personal notification.
If a nested notification is present, its user_id must agree with the envelope;
malformed or conflicting nested objects are refused.

The addressed user's multiple connections remain supported. Organization-wide
notifications must create an individual notification for each intended user.
Other events are unchanged: a user_id identifying the actor of a device update
must not silently convert that update into a personal notification.

All API processes that serve the event WebSocket must run this correction.
Mixed old/new API processes can still disclose personal notifications through
an old process. This filter is independent of Redis/Valkey fanout and applies
when the actual event router sends each frame.

Qualification includes regressions against the previous source, related event
and notification tests, and actual Redis/Valkey-to-WebSocket transport through
two independent receiver processes. The transport fixture uses synthetic
already-authenticated connection state; it does not prove login, token
revalidation, production tenant data, browser rendering or hardware behavior.
Those are separate acceptance gates.

Limits: notification transaction ownership, durable event/retry publication,
crash recovery, stale connection authorization between revalidations and
exactly-once delivery are not solved by this recipient filter. No database
migration or notification-outbox implementation is part of this change.
